{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block content %}
<section style="padding-top:30px;">
  <span class="tape">Read this first</span>
  <h1>How to score 90%+ in CHSE +2 Physics</h1>
  <p class="sub" style="text-align:left;font-size:1.1rem;color:var(--ink-soft);max-width:none;">
    Notes alone don't get marks — how you use them and how you write in the exam hall does. This page is the
    strategy layer that sits on top of every chapter.
  </p>
</section>

<div class="chapter-content" style="margin-bottom:40px;">

  <section class="topic-section">
    <h2><span class="sec-no">1</span> Know exactly how the paper is scored</h2>
    <p>CHSE +2 Physics (Class XI &amp; XII) theory is out of <b>70 marks</b>, plus <b>30 marks for practicals</b> —
      100 total. The official syllabus document splits theory questions into three cognitive levels:</p>
    <div class="formula-box">
      <div class="f-title">📐 Official CHSE question typology (Theory, 70 marks)</div>
      <div class="f-line">Remembering + Understanding — 27 marks (≈38%)</div>
      <div class="f-line">Applying — 22 marks (≈32%)</div>
      <div class="f-line">Analysing + Evaluating + Creating — 21 marks (≈30%)</div>
    </div>
    <p>This means roughly <mark class="hl-yellow">a third of the paper</mark> is straightforward recall/definitions,
      a third is direct numerical/formula application, and a third genuinely tests deeper understanding — derivations,
      comparisons, "explain why" questions, and multi-step problems. <b>You cannot hit 90% on recall alone</b> —
      the Exam Corner in every chapter is built around exactly this 1/2/3/5-mark split so you practice all three levels.</p>
    <div class="tip-box">💡 <b>Score booster:</b> Don't skip the 30-mark practical component in your planning — a strong practical + viva score is often the easiest place to recover marks lost in theory.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-section">
    <h2><span class="sec-no">2</span> A study order that actually works</h2>
    <ol class="note-list">
      <li><b>First pass (concept building):</b> Read the chapter notes fully — every diagram, formula box, and sticky note. Don't skip the "common mistakes" callouts; they're placed exactly where students lose marks.</li>
      <li><b>Second pass (derivation drill):</b> Close the notes and try to reproduce every derivation from memory on paper. CHSE 5-mark questions are almost always a derivation or a derivation + numerical combo.</li>
      <li><b>Third pass (numerical fluency):</b> Redo every solved example without looking at the steps first. Numerical questions are marked step-by-step in CHSE — method marks matter even if your final number is slightly off due to rounding.</li>
      <li><b>Fourth pass (exam simulation):</b> Attempt the Exam Corner questions chapter by chapter, under time pressure, without notes.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-section">
    <h2><span class="sec-no">3</span> Answer-writing habits that earn method marks</h2>
    <ul class="note-list">
      <li>For derivations: always start with a one-line statement of what you're proving and a labelled diagram if relevant — examiners award marks for the diagram separately from the maths.</li>
      <li>For numericals: write the given data and the formula <i>before</i> substituting numbers. If your final answer is wrong but your method and formula are correct, you still collect most of the marks.</li>
      <li>Always carry units through every step of a calculation, and box your final answer with correct units.</li>
      <li>For "distinguish between" questions, answer in a two-column comparison rather than two separate paragraphs — it is faster to write and easier for the examiner to award full marks.</li>
      <li>For 1-mark questions, answer in one line — don't over-write and lose time you need elsewhere.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="mistake-box">⚠️ <b>Don't lose marks:</b> Leaving out the unit in a final numerical answer is one of the most common and easily avoidable mark deductions in CHSE evaluation.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-section">
    <h2><span class="sec-no">4</span> Time management in the 3-hour exam</h2>
    <p>With 70 marks in 3 hours, aim for roughly <b>2.5 minutes per mark</b>, leaving a buffer to review. A practical
      pacing plan:</p>
    <ul class="note-list">
      <li>Spend the first 5 minutes reading the full paper and marking questions you're most confident about.</li>
      <li>Attempt 1 and 2-mark questions first — they're quick wins that build momentum.</li>
      <li>Attempt 5-mark derivation/long-answer questions next, while your mind is fresh.</li>
      <li>Leave 10–15 minutes at the end purely for rechecking numerical answers and units.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-section">
    <h2><span class="sec-no">5</span> Use every tool on this site</h2>
    <ul class="note-list">
      <li><b>Derivation boxes (teal):</b> the exact step-by-step logic CHSE examiners expect to see, in the right order.</li>
      <li><b>Solved example boxes (orange):</b> fully worked numericals showing how marks are distributed across steps.</li>
      <li><b>Mistake callouts (red):</b> the specific slip-ups that cost real students real marks — read these twice.</li>
      <li><b>Exam Corner (purple):</b> practice questions grouped by mark value, in CHSE's own style, at the bottom of every chapter.</li>
      <li><b>Formula sheets:</b> a consolidated, chapter-by-chapter list of every formula for rapid last-week revision — see
        <a href="class11/formula-sheet.html">Class XI Formula Sheet</a> and
        <a href="class12/formula-sheet.html">Class XII Formula Sheet</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="recap-box">
    <h3>⚡ The one-line summary</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Understand the marking split (38/32/30) and practice all three levels, not just recall.</li>
      <li>Derivations and solved numericals win the most marks per minute of study time — prioritise them.</li>
      <li>Write answers the way examiners award marks: labelled diagrams, formula before substitution, units always.</li>
      <li>Practice under timed conditions using the Exam Corner before the real exam.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
